From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hermes <hermes@local>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 10:46:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] host: the handshake's own bytes are in the log, not just
 their first sixteen
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Log 8931D405 (patch 0148, the budget at 250 ms) is the same stall as 0147, and it closes both
explanations this thread has been living on. The budget is not the variable: 2000 ms, 2001 ms and
251 ms all end with one send for the session (the 213-byte ClientHello), the server's 1375-byte
flight read once, and then nothing but a poll asking for POLLIN every five seconds until the title
shuts the socket down. And the errno is not read at all: the C++ GetLastErrno spelling, the one
answer that used to leave no line, now prints when it is asked and appears zero times in the whole
log. So the title, having read the flight, is waiting to read a record that is not coming, while the
server waits for the flight the title never writes.

What no log so far can say is why, because every one of them carries sixteen bytes of the ClientHello
and sixteen of the server's flight: enough to name a TLS record, not enough to read the offer
(versions, cipher list, extensions beyond SNI/ALPN) or to compare the flight with the one the same
offer gets from this host. Both fit in the log — 208 bytes and 1375 — so they are dumped in hex, once
per direction and capped, and the flow lines now carry the calling thread (a handshake that stops
between a read and a write is a different bug when one thread owns both) and the empty read names the
length it asked for and the flags it used.

Upstream-Status: fork-only
Reason: Log 8931D405 (patch 0148) repeats 0146/0147 exactly with the read budget at 251 ms, so neither the budget
  nor the errno can be the cause: the title sends one ClientHello, reads the server's whole flight, and then only
  polls for readability until it shuts the socket down, and the freshly traced C++ GetLastErrno is never called
  (zero lines in the log). With the socket API answered the same way in the build that wrote its flight (0144) and
  in the four that do not, the next step needs the bytes themselves: the whole ClientHello and the whole flight in
  hex, once per direction, plus the calling thread on every flow line and the asked length on an empty read, so the
  handshake can be replayed and compared here instead of guessed at.
Issue: https://github.com/GigiFrack/NX2Apple (fork)
---
 dm/host/hle_net.c | 39 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dm/host/hle_net.c b/dm/host/hle_net.c
index d2590b35..3dbe665f 100644
--- a/dm/host/hle_net.c
+++ b/dm/host/hle_net.c
@@ -585,6 +585,28 @@ static void nd_msg_trace(const char *what, int fd, unsigned iov, long bytes, int
         nd_h2_frames(what, buf, got);
     }
 }
+// The handshake's own bytes, whole and once per direction. Every log so far carries sixteen bytes of the
+// ClientHello and sixteen of the server's flight, which is enough to say "a TLS record" and not enough to
+// answer the two questions the stall leaves: what the title offered (versions, ciphers, extensions, ALPN),
+// and whether the flight it received is the one the same offer gets from this host. Both fit in a few dozen
+// lines of hex, and a log already carries thousands, so the next run can be replayed here instead of guessed
+// at. Capped, once: a session that works must not pay for this.
+static void nd_hex_dump(const char *dir, const void *buf, size_t n) {
+    static int once_send, once_recv;
+    int *once = dir[0] == 's' ? &once_send : &once_recv;
+    if (*once || !buf || !n) return;
+    *once = 1;
+    size_t cap = n < 1400 ? n : 1400;
+    char line[2 * 32 + 1];
+    fprintf(stderr, "[dm] nextendo: %s hex: %zu byte(s)%s\n", dir, n, n > cap ? ", first 1400" : "");
+    for (size_t at = 0; at < cap; at += 32) {
+        size_t k = cap - at < 32 ? cap - at : 32;
+        int o = 0;
+        for (size_t i = 0; i < k; i++)
+            o += snprintf(line + o, sizeof line - (size_t)o, "%02x", ((const unsigned char *)buf)[at + i]);
+        fprintf(stderr, "[dm] nextendo: %s hex %04zu: %s\n", dir, at, line);
+    }
+}
 // The same for every read and write that reaches a stream socket, whichever spelling the title used: the
 // gRPC transport writes its preface through the vectored call, and the TLS library under it may write
 // through send(2). A trace that reads only the first of those shows a connection that never spoke.
@@ -598,12 +620,17 @@ static void nd_flow_trace(const char *dir, int fd, long bytes, const void *buf,
         pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_nd_tcp_m);
     }
     if (n > 24 && n % 16) return;
-    char t[80]; snprintf(t, sizeof t, "socket::%s(fd %d)", dir, fd);
+    // The thread is part of the line: a handshake that stops between a read and a write is a different bug
+    // when the two come from one thread than when a second thread owes the write.
+    char t[96];
+    snprintf(t, sizeof t, "socket::%s(fd %d) t%03x", dir, fd,
+             (unsigned)((uintptr_t)pthread_self() >> 8) & 0xfff);
     dm_nd_trace_result(t, bytes);
     if (bytes > 0 && buf && len) {
         size_t got = (size_t)bytes < len ? (size_t)bytes : len;
         nd_head_trace(dir, buf, got);
         nd_tls_summary(dir, buf, got);
+        nd_hex_dump(dir, buf, got);
     }
 }
 
@@ -651,8 +678,14 @@ long dm_nd_recvmsg(int fd, void *msg, int flags) {
             long r2 = dm_nd_tcp_recv(fd, v->base, v->len, flags, m->name, m->name ? &again : NULL);
             if (r2 >= 0) { r = r2; named = again; }
         }
-        fprintf(stderr, "[dm] nextendo: socket::RecvMsg(fd %d) read %ld byte(s) after %ld ms (%s=%d ms, title socket %s)\n",
-                fd, r, nd_now_ms() - t0, ND_READ_ENV, budget, nd_tcp_nonblock(fd) ? "non-blocking" : "blocking");
+        // The asked length and the flags belong on the line too: a title whose read offered room for the whole
+        // flight and a title that asked for a prefix of it are different problems, and so is a read that asked
+        // not to wait.
+        fprintf(stderr, "[dm] nextendo: socket::RecvMsg(fd %d) read %ld of %zu asked, flags 0x%x, after %ld ms, "
+                        "t%03x (%s=%d ms, title socket %s)\n",
+                fd, r, v->len, flags, nd_now_ms() - t0,
+                (unsigned)((uintptr_t)pthread_self() >> 8) & 0xfff,
+                ND_READ_ENV, budget, nd_tcp_nonblock(fd) ? "non-blocking" : "blocking");
     }
     if (r >= 0) { if (m->name) m->name_len = named; m->flags = 0; }
     static int seen;
